            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Gregor Mendel - Monk and Geneticist</strong><br>\nGregor Mendel was an Austrian monk and scientist who is known as the father of modern genetics. He conducted hybridization experiments with pea plants in the garden of his monastery which led to the discovery of the fundamental laws of inheritance. Mendel's work was revolutionary because he was the first to apply statistical methods to biology, predicting how traits would pass from one generation to the next.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nBorn Johann Mendel in 1822, Gregor Mendel was raised in a German-speaking family in the Silesian part of the Austrian Empire. He joined the Augustinian Abbey of St. Thomas in Brno in 1843, where he adopted the name Gregor. Mendel studied at the University of Vienna and gained expertise in plant breeding and the study of variation in plants.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mendel's Experiments</strong><br>\nFrom 1856 to 1863, Mendel cultivated and tested some 29,000 pea plants. He focused on seven characteristics of pea plants, including plant height and pod shape. This research allowed him to formulate the concept of genes as the units of heredity, though the term \"gene\" was introduced later.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Laws of Inheritance</strong><br>\nMendel's work led to the establishment of three laws of inheritance: the Law of Segregation, the Law of Independent Assortment, and the Law of Dominance. These principles explained how traits are passed on and vary in offspring, establishing the patterns of genetic inheritance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mendel's Publications and Recognition</strong><br>\nMendel published his findings in 1866 in the \"Proceedings of the Natural History Society of Brunn.\" However, his work remained largely unknown to the scientific community until it was rediscovered in the early 20th century, years after his death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Challenges and Criticisms</strong><br>\nMendel faced skepticism and disbelief as his results were statistically too perfect according to some later critics. This led to some controversy regarding the validity of his data. However, subsequent validation of his laws has firmly established his legacy in genetics.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
